<p>Steel bar, pipe, timber, conduit and cable are bought in lengths or rolls and sold by the metre or foot. HardPoint already counts
  them in metres. With <strong>Cut lengths and offcuts</strong> it also knows how that stock is made up: so many full lengths, and
  the pieces left over from earlier cuts. The till then tells whoever is cutting which piece to cut from, and stops a sale when
  there's enough in all but no single piece long enough.</p>
<p class="hint">Don't see it? It's one of the optional features: the owner switches on <strong>Cut lengths and offcuts</strong> in <strong>Settings › Features</strong>.</p>

<h2>Setting a product up</h2>
<p>Open the product, press <strong>Edit</strong>, and under <strong>More options</strong> fill in:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Comes in lengths of</strong>: how long a full length or roll is, in the unit the product is sold by (6 for a 6 m bar, 100 for a 100 m roll of cable).</li>
  <li><strong>Offcuts shorter than this are scrap</strong>: a piece left over that's shorter than this is written off as cutting waste
    instead of being kept. Leave it blank to keep every offcut.</li>
</ul>
<p>The product has to be sold by a measured unit (metres, feet) and track stock. Stock you already have is counted as full lengths, with
  anything over as one part length.</p>

<h2>At the till</h2>
<p>Tap the product and the till asks for <strong>Pieces</strong> and <strong>Length each</strong>: 3 pieces of 2.5 m goes on the sale as
  7.5 m, shown as "3 × 2.5 m" on the sale and the receipt. Under the boxes it says what's on the rack here: the full lengths, and the
  offcuts with their lengths, so you can answer "do you have a 3 metre piece?" without walking to the yard. Change the pieces or the
  length on the sale's line at any time before payment. Scanning the product's barcode adds one full length.</p>
<p>If no length or offcut is long enough for a piece, the till says so and gives the longest there is, even when the metres in stock
  would cover it: four 2 m offcuts aren't an 8 m piece.</p>

<h2>What to cut from</h2>
<p>When the sale is paid, the green box at the top of the till lists <strong>To cut</strong>, for example "2.5 m cut from a 3.5 m offcut,
  leaving a 1 m offcut". HardPoint always uses the shortest piece that's long enough, so short offcuts go first and full lengths are
  only cut when nothing else will do. The same list stays on the sale's page and in the product's stock history.</p>

<h2>The offcuts on the rack</h2>
<p>The product's page has <strong>Lengths and offcuts</strong>: for each branch, how many full lengths there are and each offcut. Anyone
  who manages stock can:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Add offcut</strong>: list a piece that was on the rack before HardPoint kept track. The stock doesn't change, only how it's counted.</li>
  <li><strong>Scrap</strong>: an offcut that was thrown away or is too bent to sell. It comes out of stock as cutting waste.</li>
  <li><strong>Not an offcut</strong>: take a piece off the list that was put there by mistake. The stock stays.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cutting waste appears in the stock history and in the profit and loss report with the other stock write-offs.</p>

<h2>Quotes and orders</h2>
<p>On a quote or order, enter the total as the quantity and the length of each piece under <strong>Cut into pieces of</strong>: 7.5 cut
  into pieces of 2.5 is three pieces. The quote shows "3 × 2.5 m", and when the order is collected it arrives at the till cut that way.
  Leave it blank to sell by the metre.</p>

<h2>When the internet is down</h2>
<p>The offline till asks for pieces and a length in the same way, and sends the cut with the sale when it's back online. It can't
  see the rack, so it doesn't check that a piece is long enough; the sale is recorded as rung up.</p>

<h2>Returns, transfers and stock takes</h2>
<p>A cut piece that comes back (a return, or a sale voided) goes on the rack as an offcut of the length it was cut to. A transfer
  sends full lengths first; offcuts that no longer fit in what's left behind go with it and are listed at the branch that receives
  them. Stock takes work in metres as before; if a count leaves less stock than the offcuts add up to, the shortest offcuts are
  dropped from the list.</p>

<h2>Spreadsheets and the online store</h2>
<p>The product spreadsheet has <strong>stock_length</strong> and <strong>scrap_below</strong> columns, so a whole steel list can be set up
  by import. The online store sells these products by the metre; set the cut on the order when the customer says how they want it.</p>
